WEBVTT

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:07.013
So, our game is looking
really good now, so

00:00:07.025 --> 00:00:11.160
we have the background
that scrolls endlessly.

00:00:11.161 --> 00:00:13.522
We have our happy player
who jumps through the

00:00:13.534 --> 00:00:15.960
forest as they're avoiding
all these obstacles.

00:00:15.961 --> 00:00:18.716
And now what we want to do
is we want to actually make it so

00:00:18.717 --> 00:00:25.120
that we can end our game and
send it to a game over screen.

00:00:25.121 --> 00:00:27.471
So what we're going to
do is we need to figure

00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:29.640
out how we can
actually label all of these

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.490
different objects so
that whenever our player

00:00:32.502 --> 00:00:35.080
collides with them we
know what we've hit.

00:00:35.081 --> 00:00:39.760
One really cool way of doing this
is by using something called tags.

00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:42.242
So if you look at the
top right of our inspector,

00:00:42.254 --> 00:00:47.320
we have our ground selected and
there's this little button called tag.

00:00:47.321 --> 00:00:50.640
And what we can actually do is
create different tags for different things.

00:00:50.641 --> 00:00:53.418
So we can actually go
and tag our player as

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:56.480
player, tag our main
camera as our main camera,

00:00:56.481 --> 00:00:59.320
we can tag locations,
different things like that.

00:00:59.321 --> 00:01:04.920
So we'll actually create our own tag
by clicking add tag under that dropdown.

00:01:04.921 --> 00:01:08.120
And you'll see that there's a
tag here that says list is empty.

00:01:08.121 --> 00:01:12.280
So this is our list of tags that
we can use that we created.

00:01:12.281 --> 00:01:13.280
We haven't made any yet.

00:01:13.281 --> 00:01:19.360
So we'll actually go to this little plus
sign on the bottom right of this bar.

00:01:19.509 --> 00:01:25.000
And we'll click that and we'll add our
own tag, so we'll call this one ground.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.080
And then we can press enter to save.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:36.880
And now when we go back to
our ground, oh, that's weird, huh.

00:01:36.881 --> 00:01:42.200
So what do you actually have to do is you
go to the tags in your inspector, you click

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:44.378
the dropdown and it brings
up all the different tags

00:01:44.379 --> 00:01:46.921
and then you select the
tag that you want to use.

00:01:47.319 --> 00:01:51.880
So it doesn't unfortunately automatically
tag something when you create the tag.

00:01:51.881 --> 00:01:53.506
So you just have to
make sure you go back into

00:01:53.507 --> 00:01:57.200
whatever you want to tag
and apply the appropriate one.

00:01:57.201 --> 00:01:59.520
We're going to do the
same thing to our obstacles.

00:01:59.521 --> 00:02:01.904
But in this case, since
we have prefabs, we

00:02:01.916 --> 00:02:04.480
need to double click
on our obstacles or click

00:02:04.481 --> 00:02:08.560
on the obstacle and
click on the open button.

00:02:08.561 --> 00:02:11.039
And then you can see
we have another tag here.

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:18.240
So we'll go to click it and it's on
tag right now as we'll add a tag.

00:02:18.241 --> 00:02:20.920
And we already have our
ground tag, but we want a new tag.

00:02:20.921 --> 00:02:22.920
So we'll create another one.

00:02:22.921 --> 00:02:27.960
And we'll call this one obstacle.

00:02:27.961 --> 00:02:31.235
Now if we go back to
our hierarchy that shows

00:02:31.247 --> 00:02:35.681
us our prefab preview, you
can see the obstacle here.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.316
So if we click that and
then under tags, just

00:02:39.328 --> 00:02:41.800
make sure that you
go and tag an obstacle.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:44.578
And then when we
leave our prefab editor by

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:50.600
using the arrow next to the
obstacle underneath our hierarchy.

00:02:50.601 --> 00:02:53.269
And whenever we
create obstacles, it'll have

00:02:53.281 --> 00:02:55.840
this tag applied to
it that says obstacle.

00:02:55.841 --> 00:03:00.040
So now we need an actual way
to interact with these obstacles.

00:03:00.041 --> 00:03:03.760
So we're actually just going to
use our player controller script.

00:03:03.761 --> 00:03:06.570
And then in our player
controller script, you can see

00:03:06.571 --> 00:03:10.600
that we actually already
have a way to detect collisions.

00:03:10.601 --> 00:03:14.360
In this case, we're detecting
a collision on the ground.

00:03:14.361 --> 00:03:16.888
So in our player controller
class, this is actually

00:03:16.889 --> 00:03:19.600
where we'll just store
the state of our game over.

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:23.560
So we'll just make a
public Boolean for now.

00:03:23.561 --> 00:03:28.360
So that way we can see when
our Boolean is true or false.

00:03:28.361 --> 00:03:33.840
And we'll name this game over.

00:03:33.841 --> 00:03:36.880
So now we have our game over state saved.

00:03:36.881 --> 00:03:38.400
And we're actually going down.

00:03:38.401 --> 00:03:40.428
And you can see that
we conveniently already

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.480
have a way to track
collisions against other

00:03:42.481 --> 00:03:46.840
things using our on
collision enter method.

00:03:46.841 --> 00:03:49.243
So this is actually the place
where we can compare the

00:03:49.244 --> 00:03:53.320
different tags whenever we
run into those objects specifically.

00:03:53.321 --> 00:03:57.600
So we'll start actually with the ground
since we already have this variable here.

00:04:00.795 --> 00:04:04.873
So if and then our
condition, we're going to use

00:04:04.885 --> 00:04:08.720
collision because in
this case, the collision

00:04:08.721 --> 00:04:11.720
is actually the object
that we're colliding with.

00:04:11.721 --> 00:04:17.920
And then we're going to call
that collisions game object.

00:04:18.045 --> 00:04:22.640
And on that game object, we can
compare the current tag that it has.

00:04:22.641 --> 00:04:24.920
And we can actually
use a string for that tag.

00:04:24.921 --> 00:04:29.640
So we named our ground ground.

00:04:29.641 --> 00:04:33.137
Now in our statement,
we can specifically check

00:04:33.149 --> 00:04:36.360
when we collide with
a certain game object.

00:04:36.361 --> 00:04:40.360
We can check that game
objects tag and see if it's ground.

00:04:40.361 --> 00:04:44.440
So now we'll take our
is on ground variable.

00:04:44.441 --> 00:04:47.240
And this is where we set it to true.

00:04:47.241 --> 00:04:49.423
And now after we've
compared for the collision

00:04:49.435 --> 00:04:53.960
of the ground, we can actually
compare for the collision of our obstacle.

00:04:53.961 --> 00:04:59.040
So we'll actually use an L statement.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:21.280
So else, if what we're colliding with
is an obstacle, then we'll do this thing.

00:05:21.281 --> 00:05:23.601
So we'll check first
if we're colliding with

00:05:23.613 --> 00:05:29.080
the ground, otherwise if what we're
colliding with is an obstacle, we'll do this.

00:05:29.081 --> 00:05:31.262
So we can use a debug.log
statement so that we can

00:05:31.263 --> 00:05:39.800
see in the editor in Unity
when our game is over.

00:05:39.801 --> 00:05:44.840
And then we have that handy
game over Boolean at the top.

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:54.640
So since its default value is
false, now we set game over to true.

00:05:54.641 --> 00:06:01.440
So when we go back into Unity, when we play
our game, you should see on the bottom left

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:08.400
that game over pops up and
tells us that we ran into an obstacle.

00:06:08.401 --> 00:06:10.480
So we'll just wait for one
of these fun obstacles.

00:06:10.481 --> 00:06:11.481
And there's one.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:12.680
Ooh, missed it.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:14.360
My farmer's just so excited.

00:06:14.361 --> 00:06:21.280
And then we'll hit this one and boom, you
can see there's a game over at the bottom.

00:06:21.281 --> 00:06:25.480
So now what you're going to do is you're
going to make sure on your ground that you

00:06:25.481 --> 00:06:30.040
add a ground tag by using
the dropdown, adding a tag.

00:06:30.041 --> 00:06:32.440
And then in this list, creating a new tag.

00:06:32.441 --> 00:06:37.840
And then what you're going to do is you're
going to go into your obstacle prefab.

00:06:37.841 --> 00:06:44.640
And then you're going to apply a
tag to this as well called obstacle.

00:06:44.641 --> 00:06:51.080
And then just make sure that you apply
the tag before you leave the prefab editor.

00:06:51.081 --> 00:06:56.800
And then in your player controller script,
you're going to create a game over variable

00:06:56.801 --> 00:07:01.840
so that we know when the
game is over or when it is not.

00:07:01.841 --> 00:07:04.242
And then we're going
to change some of our

00:07:04.254 --> 00:07:06.840
code in our on collision
enter method so that

00:07:06.841 --> 00:07:14.160
we have an if statement to see if the object
that we collide with is tagged as ground,

00:07:15.055 --> 00:07:22.880
then we can set is on ground to true.

00:07:22.881 --> 00:07:23.992
Otherwise, if the object
we collide with is an

00:07:24.004 --> 00:07:25.080
obstacle, then we set
our game over to true.

00:07:25.081 --> 00:07:30.280
And we tell our editor in unity
that the game is now over.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:31.630
Now it's your turn.